## ===========================================================================
## GRUBBER'S MARKET — the chapter's one place, as one import
##
## The market is ATOMIC: its seventeen rooms, the stalls that furnish them, the
## apple that ends the walk, and the stallkeepers who fill it all arrive
## together, because none of them is meaningful without the others. The fruit
## stall exists to be stolen from; the alley exists as the walk's exit; the
## stallkeepers exist as the texture the walk moves through.
##
## What is NOT written here: major NPCs. Teisha stands in this market and keeps
## her own file (`npc-teisha`), because her tree splits by occasion and carries
## into the chase — she outlives the place. The market IMPORTS her, at the end
## of this file (imports nest, ADR-251 D5 as amended 2026-08-22), so the place
## owns its people in the import graph as the fiction says it does.
##
## ORDER IS SEMANTIC. An import is a paste at the import site (ADR-251 D4), and
## within this file the sections paste in source order: the rooms first, then
## what stands in them, then who does, then Teisha. Later declarations
## arbitrate against earlier ones, so moving a section is a behavioural change,
## not a tidy-up.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE MARKET
##
## Seventeen rooms here: the alley, fourteen market locations, and the two
## stages of the centre post; the eighteenth, the storehouse roof above the
## alley, is Book 1's own and lives with its scene in `eavesdrop.chord`.
## Geometry and room text are the source's, converted from `story.ni:1474,
## 2206-3005, 3623, 3666`. The market is a ring of stalls around a hub, and
## almost every stall names its own exits in its description, so the exit
## lists below are checkable against the prose beside them.
##
## The P-8 "seen from elsewhere" layer (the source's `distant description`,
## `Adjacent Rooms`) lives in `peering.chord`, which records the spike's
## answer — one phrase per target room, one dispatch line per (room,
## direction) pair.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## The Alley carries Book 1's scene (`eavesdrop.chord`): the voices at its
## mouth after the bite, the crates up to the roof, and the sweep's opening
## on the first climb back down once the story is `hunted`. Southeast is
## refused while the voices are there; up is refused while the pair is.

create the Alley
  a room
  aka narrow alley
  southeast to the Northwest Junction
  up to the Outer Market Roof
  southeast is blocked while the voices is here: alley-stay-hidden
  up is blocked while the wandering mercenaries is here: alley-ankle-grab

  This narrow alley is tucked away between a storehouse on one side and the
  canvas wall of a stallkeeper's tent on the other. It serves primarily as
  somewhere out-of-the-way for stallkeepers to sweep their garbage, so it's not
  exactly the most pleasant-smelling place in all of Miradania, but at least no
  one is likely to bother you here. You can leave to the southeast.

  phrase detail while the voices is here:
    A group of people have gathered just outside the alley. You can't quite
    hear what they're saying from here, but there is a grim, ugly tone to them
    that speaks of dangerous doings.

  after the player entering while hunted, once
    phrase roof-tumble
    move the wandering mercenaries here
    change the wandering mercenaries to approaching
    start the wandering mercenaries' lunge
    reset the player's waiting
  end after

## THE NORTH ROAD (change document, "The market gates", ruled 2026-08-24): the
## gates stand open through the calm walk and Jack is deflected, not blocked —
## the deflection line is David's (2026-08-24). Once the sweep starts the gates
## are closed and locked as in the source (`story.ni:1749`), and the refusal is
## Gentry's (`story.ni:2250`). All three arms are wired: blocked lines on one
## direction compose in declaration order, first condition that holds wins
## (GH #315, fixed 2026-08-24). The gates themselves (`story.ni:2236`) stand
## below as scenery: their "certainly never closed" description grows its
## "Until now." tail once the sweep has begun, the junction reports them
## closed, and opening, entering, or pushing them then gets the locked line.

create the Northwest Junction
  a room
  aka junction, northwest corner
  northwest to the Alley
  northeast to the Grocery Stall
  south to the Herb Stall
  east to the Candlemaker's Stall
  southeast to the Rope Stall
  north is blocked while calm: north-deflected
  north is blocked while the player's market-escape has started: escape-north
  north is blocked while hunted: gates-locked
  north is blocked while chase: gates-locked
  northwest is blocked while the player's market-escape has started: escape-alley

  This is the northwest corner of Grubber's Market. You can skirt around the
  edge of the market to the northeast or south, or head into the thick of it to
  the east or southeast. A wide, paved road leads north. You can also duck back
  into the alley to the northwest.

  phrase detail while hunted or chase:
    The north gates leading to Lord's Road are closed.

create the market gates
  aka north gates, north gate, gates, gate, ceremonial gates, wooden doors, doors
  scenery, plural
  in the Northwest Junction

  The north gates are huge wooden doors, nearly ten feet wide each, that date
  all the way back to when Toresal was a fortified town. They're kept around
  for ceremony, mostly; they're certainly never closed.

  phrase detail while hunted or chase:
    Until now.

  on the player opening while hunted or chase
    refuse gates-locked
  end on

  on the player entering while hunted or chase
    refuse gates-locked
  end on

  on the player pushing while hunted or chase
    refuse gates-locked
  end on

create the Grocery Stall
  a room
  aka grocer, grocers stall
  southwest to the Northwest Junction
  southeast to the Fruit Stall
  south to the Candlemaker's Stall
  states: trading, blocked

  This is a typical grocer's stall, with various kinds of food set out. There
  are baskets bristling with loaves of bread, yellow pyramids of cheese, and
  spiced jerky hanging in bundles overhead. You can continue along the market's
  outer ring to the southwest or southeast, or head in towards the center of the
  market to the south.

  after the player entering while the Grocery Stall is blocked and the player's market-escape has not started
    phrase stall-blocked-bounce
    move the player to a random adjacent room
  end after

## `blocked` is the source's own stall state (`story.ni:1823`): a noisy theft
## sets it and coming back bounces her (`wares.chord`, "THE THEFT RULE").
## The source cleared every stall when Market Escape began (`story.ni:4136`);
## here the bounce simply stands down while that spool runs — the same
## observable behaviour, since the chapter ends at Commerce Street. Seven
## stalls carry the state: the Fruit Stall has its own rule and is never
## blocked, and nothing at the Hat and Gems stalls can be nicked.

## The description's final sentence is David's (2026-08-24, change document
## "The escape disguise" ruling): it restores the source's shadowy-recesses
## shape and carries the tentpole-wire hint that seeds the slide's landing
## geography during the calm walk. It replaced the earlier exit-listing
## adaptation ("Aisles between the stalls lead northwest, west, southwest,
## and south.").

create the Fruit Stall
  a room
  aka fruit
  northwest to the Grocery Stall
  west to the Candlemaker's Stall
  southwest to the Pottery Stall
  south to the Eastern Junction
  northeast is blocked: behind-stall-no-entry
  states: orderly, chaotic

  Bins heaped high with brightly colored fruit lend a pleasant fragrance to this end
  of the market (though by afternoon it will have become a cloying reek that
  attracts more flies than customers). They have apples and pears from the city
  orchards, and brambleberries from the northern counties. Oranges, limes, kello
  fruit — even a bushel of bananas from the Kozar Delta. Aisles between the
  bins wind their way back into shadowy recesses of the stall, with a tentpole
  wire stretching overhead from market center.

  phrase detail while calm:
    The bushel of bananas is currently blocked by the stallkeeper.

  after the player entering while Jack is dressed
    start the player's exposure
  end after

## Behind Fruit Stall surfaces only from the let-go drop (change document,
## "Where she changes", amended 2026-08-23) — but every plain exit is
## bidirectional at load (`connectRooms` stamps both directions; ADR-234
## D4's `, one-way` is reserved and not wired, GH #327), so its `southwest
## to the Fruit Stall` minted a northeast way in. Found in play 2026-08-29:
## `ne` from here walked straight into the hidden spot. Blocked until the
## language has a one-way exit; the refusal line is David's.

define phrase behind-stall-no-entry
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. No way in behind the stall from the front.)
end phrase

create the Eastern Junction
  a room
  aka eastern edge
  north to the Fruit Stall
  south to the Hat Stall
  west to the Pottery Stall
  east to Commerce Street
  east is blocked while calm: east-deflected
  east is blocked while Jack is urchin: east-watched

  You're near the eastern edge of Grubber's Market. Commerce Street lies to the east.
  You can travel along the outer ring of stalls to the north or south, or
  head towards the market's central hub to the west.

create the Hat Stall
  a room
  aka hats, hatter
  north to the Eastern Junction
  northwest to the Pottery Stall
  west to Outside the Silk Tent
  southwest to the Leather Stall

  Hats of all styles and sizes hang from scores of wooden pegs, a forest of
  varied headgear. They have big, flop-brimmed traveling hats; small, leather
  workman's caps; wide sappans of woven straw from the Kozar delta; fancy,
  feathered hats for courtly balls; and even a few ordinary bonnets. More stalls
  lie to the north, northwest, west, and southwest.

create the Leather Stall
  a room
  aka leather, leather shop, leather goods
  northeast to the Hat Stall
  northwest to Outside the Silk Tent
  west to the Weaponsmith's Stall
  states: trading, blocked

  This merchant mostly deals in riding equipment — reins, harnesses, stirrups,
  and the like. A full riding saddle sits on proud display near the back. There
  are a few scabbards and belts for sale, as well. More stalls lie to the west, northwest,
  and northeast.

  after the player entering while the Leather Stall is blocked and the player's market-escape has not started
    phrase stall-blocked-bounce
    move the player to a random adjacent room
  end after

create the Weaponsmith's Stall
  a room
  aka weapons stall, weapons, weaponsmith, smith
  east to the Leather Stall
  northeast to Outside the Silk Tent
  northwest to the Exotic Gems Stall
  states: trading, blocked

  The weaponsmith's wares hang along the sides of his stall, a grim picket fence
  of down-hanging blades. You can find less intimidating stalls to the east, northeast,
  and northwest.

  after the player entering while the Weapons Stall is blocked and the player's market-escape has not started
    phrase stall-blocked-bounce
    move the player to a random adjacent room
  end after

create the Exotic Gems Stall
  a room
  aka gems stall, gems, jewels, jeweler
  north to the Herb Stall
  northeast to the Rope Stall
  east to Outside the Silk Tent
  southeast to the Weaponsmith's Stall

  Dozens of jewels in exotic colors and cuts are spread out on display here,
  glittering in the dusty sunlight. Some of them are loose stones, others are set in
  finely wrought rings and necklaces. Most of them were probably smuggled in
  from over the western mountains, which is why they're cheap enough to be
  affordable to anyone in Grubber's Market. Still, they're far, far out of your
  price range. Other stalls lie to the north, northeast, east, and southeast.

  on every turn while hunted and the monkey is hidden and the player is in the Exotic Gems Stall
    change the monkey to loose
    move the monkey to the Top of the Post
    phrase monkey-commotion
  end on

create the Herb Stall
  a room
  aka herbs, herbalist
  north to the Northwest Junction
  east to the Rope Stall
  south to the Exotic Gems Stall
  states: trading, blocked

  The herb stall is usually a quiet place. Customers here like to browse, poke
  their noses into the various jars, and judge the potency of a potential
  purchase at their leisure. Other stalls are to the south, and east, and the
  market opens out a little to the north.

  after the player entering while the Herb Stall is blocked and the player's market-escape has not started
    phrase stall-blocked-bounce
    move the player to a random adjacent room
  end after

create the Rope Stall
  a room
  aka rope, rope dealer, ropemaker
  northwest to the Northwest Junction
  northeast to the Candlemaker's Stall
  west to the Herb Stall
  southwest to the Exotic Gems Stall
  southeast to Outside the Silk Tent
  states: trading, blocked

  One thing you can say about the rope stall: you'll never spend a moment
  wondering what's for sale. It's all rope, and lots of it: thick and thin,
  knotted and loose, in heaps and in coils, stacked on the ground and dangling in
  loops. More stalls are to the northeast, southeast, west, and southwest, and
  the market opens out a bit to the northwest.

  after the player entering while the Rope Stall is blocked and the player's market-escape has not started
    phrase stall-blocked-bounce
    move the player to a random adjacent room
  end after

create the Candlemaker's Stall
  a room
  aka candle stall, candles, candlemaker, chandler
  north to the Grocery Stall
  east to the Fruit Stall
  west to the Northwest Junction
  southeast to the Pottery Stall
  southwest to the Rope Stall
  states: trading, blocked

  The heat and fumes from the candlemaker's tallow pots make this stall an
  unpleasant place to loiter. Still, people need candles, and customers seem to be
  buying them as fast as the stallkeeper can dip them. Most of the candles are
  tallow, fast-burning and cheap, but there are also a number of premade wax
  candles in different colors. Other stalls are to the north, east, west, southeast,
  and southwest.

  after the player entering while the Candle Stall is blocked and the player's market-escape has not started
    phrase stall-blocked-bounce
    move the player to a random adjacent room
  end after

create the Pottery Stall
  a room
  aka pottery, potter, urns
  northwest to the Candlemaker's Stall
  northeast to the Fruit Stall
  east to the Eastern Junction
  southeast to the Hat Stall
  southwest to Outside the Silk Tent
  states: trading, blocked

  The wares at the pottery stall are spread out on the ground, forcing browsers
  and passers-by to step carefully around them. The urns, jars, and bowls are each
  uniquely shaped, yet they all share a common, graceful curve. Most of them are
  made of fired clay from the Westlands, decorated with colorful glazes. There are
  more stalls to the northwest, northeast, southeast, and southwest, or you can
  head out to the market's edge to the east.

  after the player entering while the Pottery Stall is blocked and the player's market-escape has not started
    phrase stall-blocked-bounce
    move the player to a random adjacent room
  end after

create Outside the Silk Tent
  a room
  aka silk stall, outside the tent, teishas stall
  north to Inside the Silk Tent
  east to the Hat Stall
  west to the Exotic Gems Stall
  northeast to the Pottery Stall
  northwest to the Rope Stall
  southeast to the Leather Stall
  southwest to the Weaponsmith's Stall
  north is blocked while the player's market-escape has started: escape-tent-planted

  Teisha has done well enough for herself that her 'stall' is actually a full-on
  tent. You can enter the tent to the north, or move on to one of the many other
  stalls to the east, west, northeast, northwest, southeast, or southwest.

  after the player entering while the player's market-escape has started
    phrase escape-tent-planted
    move the player to a random adjacent room
  end after

create Inside the Silk Tent
  a room
  aka silk tent, tent, inside the tent
  south to Outside the Silk Tent
  south is blocked while chase: tent-no-way-back
  north to the Base of the Center Post

  The walls of the tent provide a bit of quiet and privacy from the crowds outside
  (in addition to keeping dust off the merchandise). Beautiful silk garments hang on
  either side. Gowns, robes, capes, cloaks, and shawls, the fabric rippling
  with the slightest movement of air, shimmering with that soft, liquid glow that
  only silk has. The main market lies to the south. A narrow gap to the north
  leads behind the tent.

create the Base of the Center Post
  a room
  aka center post, centre post, post, base of the post, behind the tent
  south to Inside the Silk Tent
  south is blocked while chase: base-no-way-back
  up to the Top of the Post

  This is a cramped little square formed by the backs of three stalls and Teisha's
  tent. It's barely big enough to stretch your arms out in. Right in the middle is
  the base of the huge central support post. You can duck back into Teisha's tent
  to the south.

create the Top of the Post
  a room
  aka top of the center post, top of the centre post, top
  down to the Base of the Center Post
  down is blocked while chase: top-no-way-down
  northeast is blocked: slide-barehanded
  northwest is blocked: slide-barehanded
  southeast is blocked: slide-barehanded
  southwest is blocked: slide-barehanded

  Perched atop the high central post, you can look out over the whole market
  square. To the north, Lord's Road heads out of the city towards Lord's Keep.
  Commerce Street and the rest of the city beckon to the east. In every other
  direction the city is surrounded by rolling meadows and farmlands.

  Four anchor cables stretch away in four directions: northwest, northeast, southwest,
  and southeast.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE FRUIT STALL
##
## The stall the apple and the banana both come from — which is why the change
## document's theft decision matters here ("Theft — calm theft is quiet, chase
## theft is noisy"): a source-faithful apple theft would block this stall before
## the monkey puzzle needs its banana.
##
## The displays are the source's `storage bin`s (`story.ni:2380-2390`), each
## with a corresponding item. Descriptions are Gentry's, carried verbatim. The
## apple and the banana are created without a location: nothing places them yet,
## because the taking and stealing mechanism is the next increment.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the display of apples
  aka apples, apple display
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  in the Fruit Stall

  These don't look half bad. If you hadn't stumbled into so much trouble, you'd
  be eating one right now.

create the display of pears
  aka pears, pear display
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  in the Fruit Stall

  These vary in color from yellowish-green to dusky orange-brown, with thick,
  twisty stems.

create the display of kello fruit
  aka kello, kello fruit, kello display
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  in the Fruit Stall

  Normally you'd kill for a slice of kello, but these look a bit too green. Not
  quite in season.

create the display of brambleberries
  aka brambleberries, brambleberry clusters, clusters, berries
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  in the Fruit Stall

  The thumb-sized clusters are shiny and bluish-black. Your mouth waters at the
  thought of how tart they must be.

create the display of oranges
  aka oranges, orange display, pale oranges, small oranges
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  in the Fruit Stall

  The oranges are pale and disappointingly small.

create the display of limes
  aka limes, lime display, dark green limes, green limes
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  in the Fruit Stall

  The dark green limes look refreshing.

create the display of bananas
  aka bananas, banana display, bushel
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  in the Fruit Stall

  You rarely see bananas this far north. These look just about perfect — plump,
  bright yellow with just a sprinkling of brown freckles.

create the apple
  aka round, firm, green apple
  edible
  states: whole, bitten
  on the display of apples

  The apple is round, firm, green at the bottom shading up to red near the stem.

  phrase detail while the apple is bitten:
    There is a big bite taken out of one side.

  after the player taking while calm, once
    phrase apple-lifted-quietly
  end after

  on the player eating
    refuse when the player is not in the Alley: apple-not-here
    phrase apple-first-bite
    change the apple to bitten
    move the voices to the Alley
    move the group of mercenaries to the Outer Market Roof
    move the mercenary captain to the Outer Market Roof
  end on

create the banana
  aka kozarian banana, yellow banana
  edible
  states: shelved, nicked
  on the display of bananas

  The banana is plump, bright yellow with just a sprinkling of brown freckles.

  on the player taking while the banana is shelved
    refuse when calm: banana-blocked
    refuse when the player's market-escape has started: escape-no-time
    phrase fruit-theft-chaos when the Fruit Stall is orderly and thefts is 0
    phrase fruit-theft-chaos-thief when the Fruit Stall is orderly and thefts is at least 1
    phrase fruit-theft-again when the Fruit Stall is chaotic
  end on

  after the player taking while the banana is shelved
    change the banana to nicked
    move the banana to the cloth satchel
    raise thefts by 1
    change the Fruit Stall to chaotic when the Fruit Stall is orderly
    move the player to a random adjacent room
  end after

## The other fruit take the same rule through the `fruit-ware` trait below —
## the banana keeps its own clause because its calm refusal is David's
## ruling and a trait's clause cannot sit beside an entity's own (GH #332).
## A fruit is `shelved` until taken and `nicked` after, so a dropped fruit
## taken again is a plain take (the source's on-stage `try taking`).

define trait fruit-ware
  on the player taking while it is shelved
    refuse when the player's market-escape has started: escape-no-time
    phrase stall-lift-quietly when calm
    phrase fruit-theft-chaos when (hunted or chase) and the Fruit Stall is orderly and thefts is 0
    phrase fruit-theft-chaos-thief when (hunted or chase) and the Fruit Stall is orderly and thefts is at least 1
    phrase fruit-theft-again when (hunted or chase) and the Fruit Stall is chaotic
  end on

  after the player taking while it is shelved
    change it to nicked
    move it to the cloth satchel when hunted or chase
    raise thefts by 1 when hunted or chase
    change the Fruit Stall to chaotic when (hunted or chase) and the Fruit Stall is orderly
    move the player to a random adjacent room when hunted or chase
  end after
end trait

## The calm-walk banana is unreachable — RULED (David, 2026-08-24): the
## stallkeeper is blocking the banana bin during the calm walk, so there is no
## quiet banana lift (that placeholder is retired). The theft happens under
## the sweep, where the source's rule applies unchanged (change document,
## "Theft"). The room's gated detail line carries David's "currently blocked
## by the stallkeeper" wording while calm (GH #316 landed: it renders on
## `look` as well as on examining).
##
## THE NOISY THEFT — the source's rule for THIS stall (`story.ni:2438-2445`,
## "Fruit Theft"), not the generic one (`story.ni:1990-1998`). Stealing fruit
## from the orderly Fruit Stall is seen and shouted, but a passing mercenary
## trips over the monkey into the bins, and in the chaos Jack slips away with
## the fruit in her satchel: the stall turns `chaotic` and is left UNBLOCKED
## — "You shouldn't have any trouble coming back here if you need to" —
## which is what the escape's landing at this stall later depends on. The
## eject is ADR-326's `move the player to a random adjacent room` (the
## blocked northeast is excluded by the draw). The clause is an on+after
## pair, the trade trait's shape: the `on` phrase replaces stdlib's "You
## take the banana" as the action's message, the `after` does the moving
## once the take has really happened. Two readings flagged for
## David: (1) the change document's gloss "(seen, kept, ejected, stall
## blocked)" describes the generic rule; the source's own rule for this
## stall leaves it unblocked, and "unchanged" is read as the source's rule.
## (2) The source keys the opener on a mere-urchin/successful-thief flag the
## apple's calm lift never set (the 2009 player starts holding the apple and
## a mere urchin), so the banana gets the first-theft opener; the on-edge
## opener the change document quotes (`story.ni:1997`) is kept aside below.
## Since the wares increment (2026-08-30) every fruit sits on its display
## and takes this rule, so the chaotic-stall second theft ("no one notices
## you picking up another [noun]", `story.ni:2447`) has consumers and is
## carried as `fruit-theft-again`; the openers split on the `thefts`
## counter as the source's urchin/thief flag did (`wares.chord`). The
## generic blocked-stall bounce (`story.ni:1825`) is carried on the seven
## stalls that can be blocked.
## Known seam (ADR-326 D5: the draw narrates nothing): the eject prints the
## theft text and no destination description — `look` answers.
##
## The steal verbs (`story.ni:1954-1965`) are taking's synonyms, as the
## source resolves them ("if the noun is on-stage: try taking").

extend action taking
  grammar
    steal the item
    filch the item
    grab the item
    loot the item
    nick the item
    pilfer the item
    shoplift the item
    snatch the item
    swipe the item
    thieve the item

define phrase banana-blocked
  Unfortunately, the bin holding the bananas is too close to the stallkeeper
  to make a move.
end phrase

## The two openers are the source's own split (`story.ni:2440`): the first
## theft of the game gets "Ah, this is something you know how to do"; a
## Jack who has already been shouted at gets "With the stallkeepers all on
## edge". The fruit named is the taking action's own `{the item}` slot.

define phrase fruit-theft-chaos
  Ah, *this* is something you know how to do, at least. You sidle up casually
  to the stall, wait until the stallkeeper seems preoccupied, then reach out
  and pluck {the item} from the shelf.

  Suddenly the stallkeeper jabs his finger at you and screams, *'Stop,
  thief!'*

  One of the mercenaries happens to be wandering by at that moment. He hears
  the stallkeeper's shouts and comes running straight for you!

  Then there is a loud screech, and something gray and hairy scampers out
  from under the mercenary's feet. He stumbles, trips, and pitches headlong
  into the fruit stall. Fruit scatters everywhere. The stallkeeper is wailing
  and berating the mercenary, who is covered with juice and slimy pulp.

  In the general chaos, you slip away easily, stuffing {the item} into your
  satchel. Fortunately, no one managed to get a very good look at you, and
  the stallkeeper will be too busy cleaning up his wares to keep an eye out
  for you. You shouldn't have any trouble coming back here if you need to.
end phrase

define phrase fruit-theft-chaos-thief
  With the stallkeepers all on edge about the mercenaries, it's not likely
  you'll be able to nick anything without someone noticing. Still, nothing
  ventured...

  *'Thief!'*

  One of the mercenaries happens to be wandering by at that moment. He hears
  the stallkeeper's shouts and comes running straight for you!

  Then there is a loud screech, and something gray and hairy scampers out
  from under the mercenary's feet. He stumbles, trips, and pitches headlong
  into the fruit stall. Fruit scatters everywhere. The stallkeeper is wailing
  and berating the mercenary, who is covered with juice and slimy pulp.

  In the general chaos, you slip away easily, stuffing {the item} into your
  satchel. Fortunately, no one managed to get a very good look at you, and
  the stallkeeper will be too busy cleaning up his wares to keep an eye out
  for you. You shouldn't have any trouble coming back here if you need to.
end phrase

## `story.ni:2447` — a second fruit from the chaotic stall. "another
## [noun]" reads "another piece of fruit": a marker cannot render a bare
## name (deviation 8 in the story header's list).

define phrase fruit-theft-again
  With the stallkeeper still sorting out his spilled fruit, and most of the
  shoppers trying to avoid the mess, no one notices you picking up another
  piece of fruit. You slip the fruit into your satchel and move quickly to
  another part of the market.
end phrase

define phrase fruit-keeper-busy
  The stallkeeper is far too busy trying to clean up all the spilled fruit to
  deal with you.
end phrase

